[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Angular Error Handling with Interceptors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors in Angular provide a powerful mechanism to intercept and handle HTTP requests and responses globally. This includes handling errors in a centralized and consistent manner. This tutorial will guide you through implementing error handling using interceptors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are Interceptors?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors are classes that implement the `HttpInterceptor` interface. They sit between your application and the backend server, allowing you to modify requests before they are sent and responses before they are received. This makes them ideal for tasks like adding headers, logging, and, importantly, handling errors."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an Error Handling Interceptor"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a new Angular service that will act as our interceptor. This service will implement the `HttpInterceptor` interface."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpHandler, HttpEvent, HttpErrorResponse } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable, throwError } from 'rxjs';
import { catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class ErrorInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {

  intercept(request: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<any>> {
    return next.handle(request)
      .pipe(
        catchError((error: HttpErrorResponse) => {
          let errorMessage = '';
          if (error.error instanceof ErrorEvent) {
            // Client-side error
            errorMessage = `Error: ${error.error.message}`;
          } else {
            // Server-side error
            errorMessage = `Error Code: ${error.status}\nMessage: ${error.message}`;
          }
          console.log(errorMessage);
          return throwError(errorMessage);
        })
      )
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the `intercept` method, we use the `pipe` operator and the `catchError` operator to handle any errors that occur during the HTTP request.  The `catchError` operator allows us to intercept the error, log it, and then re-throw it so that the component that made the request can also handle it."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Registering the Interceptor"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To make the interceptor work, you need to register it in your `app.module.ts` (or relevant module) file."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { HttpClientModule, HTTP_INTERCEPTORS } from '@angular/common/http';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { ErrorInterceptor } from './error.interceptor';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule,
    HttpClientModule
  ],
  providers: [
    {
      provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
      useClass: ErrorInterceptor,
      multi: true
    }
  ],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS`:  This tells Angular that we are providing an interceptor."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`useClass: ErrorInterceptor`:  This specifies the interceptor class to use."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`multi: true`:  This allows us to register multiple interceptors.  If set to `false`, it would replace any existing interceptors."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling the Error in the Component"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The interceptor logs the error and re-throws it. Now, you need to handle the error in the component that made the HTTP request."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: '<button (click)="getData()">Get Data</button>',
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }

  ngOnInit(): void { }

  getData() {
    this.http.get('https://your-api-endpoint.com/data')
      .subscribe(
        (data) => {
          console.log('Data:', data);
        },
        (error) => {
          console.error('Error from component:', error);
          alert(error);
        }
      );
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the component's `subscribe` method, the second argument is a callback function that will be executed if an error occurs. Here, we log the error to the console and display an alert to the user."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using Interceptors for Error Handling"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Centralized Error Handling:  Errors are handled in one place, making your code more maintainable."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consistent Error Handling:  Ensures that errors are handled in a consistent way across your application."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Boilerplate Code:  Avoids repeating error handling logic in every component."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors provide a clean and efficient way to handle errors in Angular applications. By centralizing error handling logic, you can improve the maintainability and consistency of your code."}]
